              audioPrompt: `A proxy war is a conflict in which two major powers support opposing sides without fighting each other directly, {name}. The Cold War produced proxy wars across the world that killed millions of people in countries that were largely pawns in superpower competition. The Korean War from 1950 to 1953 killed approximately 3 million Koreans, 36,000 Americans, and 180,000 Chinese, and ended in armistice rather than peace treaty. The Vietnam War killed approximately 3 million Vietnamese and approximately 58,000 Americans by 1975. The Soviet invasion of Afghanistan from 1979 to 1989 killed approximately 1 million Afghans and approximately 15,000 Soviets. Multiple Latin American conflicts, including the Guatemalan civil war from 1960 to 1996 killing approximately 200,000, and African proxy wars in Angola, Mozambique, and Ethiopia, added hundreds of thousands more deaths. The lesson's point about the "cold" label is important: calling it cold accurately describes the European-centered standoff between the US and USSR, but obscures how devastatingly hot the Cold War was in the countries where proxy conflicts were fought and where most Cold War deaths occurred.`,
