              audioPrompt: `The School of the Americas was a US Army institution founded in 1946 in Panama and moved to Fort Benning, Georgia in 1984, {name}. It trained approximately 60,000 Latin American military officers across its history in counterinsurgency, intelligence, and interrogation. Many of its graduates were implicated in subsequent human rights violations, including officers responsible for major massacres and assassinations. When the school's training manuals were declassified in 1996, they explicitly endorsed torture, blackmail, and assassination as counterinsurgency tools. The school was renamed the Western Hemisphere Institute for Security Cooperation, known as WHINSEC, in 2001 with substantially revised curriculum, but the legacy remains substantial. The lesson presents the School of the Americas alongside Operation Condor and the pattern of US-backed coups and authoritarian governments as evidence that the US Cold War policy in Latin America was not primarily about defending freedom and democracy but about suppressing left-leaning governments and movements that threatened US interests, including through support for governments that practiced systematic torture and killing.`,
